## [ENT-033] | 2026-11-17 08:37 UTC | Unity | order | ref: governance/rulebook.md | see: ENT-032
Changed: on Suggi's word of 2026-11-17, about 08:33 UTC, on Military's one-drone pirate test: "Send ten instead, and fight if they are only three; but if they are too strong, we avoid pirates for now until we get stronger. Test with a strike group of ten." Military's block carries it in place of the group of three, with its limits. Everything else stands as in ENT-032 (Intel's ten far explorers for its turns of 11:15 and 15:15 UTC included). Longer than ENT-032 by those limits. Military's turn of 08:00 UTC was running when this was written and takes it at its next turn.
In force from 2026-11-17 08:37 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
STANDING GOALS (Suggi, 2026-11-16, about 22:05 UTC; his words with the spelling set right, as he asked on 2026-11-17; set and changed by him alone)
G1. The empire: "Your goal as Unity is to build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy, The Collective, and to be the most powerful presence in the game as The Collective. Standing goal."
G2. The grand fleet: "I want you to have the overall goal to get to the faction limit of 1,000, so we can build the grand fleet. This will take time: you have to do the levels 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and so on."
G3. The stations: "Explore the territory between the Frontier empire and our own. We build one or two stations in the area in between: maybe Yildun, and Aldebaran can be one too. That way we have two station locations. Also Hawthorne and Alchiba: that region can be another station. So in the medium to long term we expand to that region and keep the systems from our starting place to there all running with profitable routes and things to do."
G4. Mastery: each member "has an overarching goal to improve, learn and master their class": Military all things military, Trade trading and all its aspects, Intel scouting, Mining and Logistics theirs, the Fleet Admiral the fleet as a whole.
DIRECTION (Suggi's words since, in short; set and changed by him alone)
D1. Income first: become economically positive; fill the supply chains, craft, build bases, level up. The money of the whole fleet is the measure, not one division's (rule F31).
D2. You are the players: decide and tell, never ask for leave; everything the game offers is open (rule F28); play by scripts, not by hand (rules F21, F29).
D3. Every division researches its own class, learns what its ships must train and farm and how they level, and flies mostly that work; the divisions intertwine a little where it helps. Military trains weapons and combat, the others their own (2026-11-17; rules F23, F30, F32). Every member knows its class: its guidebook opens with how its ships are built and with its skills in the order they are levelled.
D4. Expansion in three directions, the systems he names being suggestions: south (the region of Biham and Felis), north (Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba: two bases), east (Diadem, Intercrus). What we may build is limited, so nothing stands close: an outpost, a hub or a base stands 3 or 4 systems beyond our safe space, never right at its border, and 3 or 4 systems from the next one of ours, so that each also serves the exploration further out (2026-11-17; rule F33). Outposts and hubs are built freely by that rule. At the edge of our own safe space the storage for the ventures beyond it is a Faction Lockbox at a station there. The first outpost: yes.
D5. The fleet we aim for: about 500 ships (Command 51, Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60). A new pilot is born on his word only.
D6. Reports inform him and ask nothing; the Admirals write as they always did; the Fleet Admiral explains to him. He speaks when he sees something wrong or a better way.
D7. The orders evolve: only his standing orders and goals are permanent. A task leaves the orders when it is done or has become a rule.
D8. Roles (2026-11-17; rule F16): Logistics hauls, crafts and is the fleet's base builder: it builds our outposts, stations and facilities, makes what they take, hauls their fuel and supplies and keeps them running. Trade trades, and works price gaps (arbitrage) later if possible. Intel scouts, explores and hunts. Military and Mining stand as they are. Passenger lines: perhaps later, when the empire stands.
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (rules F8, F23, F30).
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work, most of each division on work of its own class.
P2. An economy that earns (rules F22, F25, F31). A route gets no more ships than its bid, its field or its herd carries. The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space, what we craft, the surplus sold.
  Goal: the money of the whole fleet rising hour by hour; the fleet's steady income per hour above zero.
P3. Level and upgrade (rule F17): Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open the hull tiers 2 to 5.
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its class gained and its next hull step.
P4. Outposts, hubs and bases (G3, D4, rule F33). The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates what it takes; Logistics builds, supplies and keeps it; Military guards it; Intel reads it first. (a) The first outpost stands 3 or 4 systems beyond our border (task 3). Read so far at that distance: Felis in the south (4 beyond: three ore belts, iron richness 54, the largest herds); in the east Intercrus (4; iron 45; four foreign pilots met), Atlas (3; iron 66), Botein (4; iron 68) and Acrux (4; iron 64, lithium); in the west Acrab (4; iron 62). An outpost: a fee of 100,000 cr and one Outpost Kit, a lawless system, a place without a station; it brings faction storage and a fuel bunker, costs no rent, serves members only, and stands with 12,000 hull and no shield (game/rules/09-factions.md, the base builder guide; not tried). (b) Intel reads the three regions and the roads to them; the divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay. (c) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr; the reserve gathers toward it and is never sold (rule F24).
  Goal: the first outpost founded, with its place and its cost; systems read in the three regions; routes that pay beyond our border.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own. The Faction Lockbox stands at Node Beta Industrial Station since 2026-11-17 05:38 UTC: the faction's shared storage, 100,000 units; its rent, 69 cr a cycle or about 6,000 cr a day, comes from the treasury (44,862 cr), which she keeps filled. Next: a second Lockbox at The Experiment Research Station, at our border beside Ironhollow, Achernar and Ruchbah (200,000 cr, 200 steel plate, 50 circuit boards; Logistics builds it); Hamal, Pherkad and GSC-0027 have no station and lie 1 or 2 jumps from one of the two Lockboxes. Beside it: 34 more ships join the faction (free; the limit reads 50, we are 16), the haulers first. Then the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk together: 375,000 cr, 275 steel plate, 125 circuit boards (51 boards held); the Fleet Admiral decides and pays each step, Logistics' builder builds it (rule F11). The whole ladder: governance/empire.md.
  Goal: faction members of 50; the treasury's days of rent; circuit boards held against 125.
TURN (the tasks of now; each leaves the orders when it is done)
Task 1. Research your class (D3). Read the game for your own class: the official pages (https://www.spacemolt.com/docs/skills.md, the guide of your role, the help texts of your commands), the catalog (the tables `skills`, `hulls` and `items` through the tool hive with verb=sql; `skills.training` says how a skill is trained) and our own record (the tool fleet with view=levels; the table `ship_skills`). Write it down where every member finds it: your guidebook opens with the Class Card (rule F14), short and lean, before the Standing Plan: `### Ships`, how your class builds its ships (hull by tier, fit, style, in a few lines), and `### Skills`, one table of the skills of your class in the order they are levelled, the most important first (skill, why it matters, what trains it and by which of your routes, your division's level now, the next goal). The detail stays in your levelling guide. The Fleet Admiral checks every Class Card at her turn. Then fly it: most of your ships on work of your class; the work of another class is the exception, a little where it helps both (rule F32). Military: its drones train weapons and combat by hunting, guarding and the pirate test; they mine only when no herd and no post is open.
  Done when: your guidebook opens with the Class Card, each skill with how it is trained and its source; your report gives the ships of your division on work of its class before and after the turn.
Task 2. Hubs and outposts, 3 or 4 systems out (D4, rule F33): Suggi asks the group. Say in your report, under Suggestions: which system 3 or 4 jumps beyond our border would serve your division best as a hub for fuel and storage, what your ships would do from there, and what it would have to hold. P4 (a) names what is read at that distance. At the edge of our own space the storage is a Faction Lockbox (P5): say also what your division would put into the one at The Experiment and take out of it.
  Done when: your report answers the three points for your division.
Task 3. The first outpost (P4 a): Intel and Mining say which of the systems 3 or 4 jumps out holds most for the fleet and is safest, with what was read there; Military plans its guard; Logistics plans its build as routes (the kit, its way there, the fuel for its bunker, the stock hauled in). The Fleet Admiral chooses the site after your reports and gives Logistics the credits and the faction's rights to build.
  Done when: your report names your part: a place with its readings, a guard plan, or a build plan with its routes.
Task 4. Two steps open since the last entry (rule F32; game/rules/08-combat.md). (a) Write `lift_to` into every standing route of yours beyond the border: the number of ships up to which fill.py may widen it by itself. (b) A ship pulled into a fight opens at its default stance, and `auto` opens an armed ship at fire; all of ours are armed. Set it by script as your class needs (spacemolt_battle default_stance): flee for a ship that is not there to fight (rule F2).
  Done when: your report names the routes that carry `lift_to` and the default stance you set, and for which ships.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by. You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play). Plan your division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division"; sell what your ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes; build what you lack (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); answer the requests addressed to you; claim what you fill (admirals.log, rule F25). Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, judge your own routes, levels and results before you report them, and name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better and one you do not master yet.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F33); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13), the reserve (F24) and where we build (F33). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the empire with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through. Every turn: read the Admirals' reports and Feedback and fix what they name or open the door; answer every request before their next turn; keep the database, the catalog, the rules digest, the money figures, the tools and the station right; write the fleet report and lean orders (D7). Pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5), choose where each outpost and base stands and allocate what it takes, which Logistics then builds (P4, rules F31 and F33), check every guidebook's Class Card, decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date. Bring to him only what is his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. The command group levels, upgrades and earns like every division (rule F30).
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; faction members of 50 and the treasury's days of rent; the builder readied and the Lockbox at The Experiment begun; the site of the first outpost chosen and its build given to Logistics; Class Cards in five guidebooks; the orders no longer than the entry before, or the reason in its `Changed:` line.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore where a field has them, then iron. Weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. Fields beyond our space are yours to test and to widen (task 4 a). The stopgap of 2026-11-17 05:52 UTC is yours to reset: three lifts beyond the border (Schedar Belt to 45 ships, Ironhollow's two belts to 25 and 20) and the floors of four home fields lowered; a floor is there so that a field grows back. The iron belts you proposed for an outpost lie two jumps out, too close by rule F33: say with Intel which system 3 or 4 jumps out holds the fields Mining needs most (task 3).
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective: units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (rule F31); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered, inside rule F12: carbon ore at about 4 cr as far as Logistics' boards use it; silicon ore only at a price you judge fair against a field still to be found. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. A station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit. Price gaps between two stations (arbitrage) are yours to work as the books allow (D8): read both ends within the hour, one ship and one unit first. The hauling of fuel and supplies for our bases is Logistics'; what a base must buy, you buy.
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: you are the fleet's quartermaster and, from this entry, its base builder (D8, rule F16): you build our outposts, stations and facilities, make what they take, haul their fuel and supplies and keep them running. The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates the credits and materials (rule F33); inside that the build is yours. (1) Research the builder's class (task 1): the official pages on stations, factions and base building, the help of spacemolt_facility, the tables `facilities` and `facility_materials`, the tool base_plan. Your guidebook says how an outpost is deployed and supplied, what a facility at a foreign station costs to keep, and what a station of our own draws every cycle. (2) Your first build is the Faction Lockbox at The Experiment Research Station (P5). Choose one hauler as the fleet's builder. This turn it carries 200 steel plate and 50 circuit boards from Node Beta into its own storage at The Experiment and stays docked there. At 09:30 UTC the Fleet Admiral takes the hauler that holds them there into the faction, gives it the rights to build and the fee, and starts the build with it (spacemolt_facility faction_build, facility_type faction_lockbox), so that it stands at your next turn; if the game gives a member no such right, her flagship builds from what your hauler hands over. Every later build is yours by script. (3) Plan the first outpost as routes (task 3): the kit (two on offer at 74,010 cr at a player faction's station in lhs_1140, six jumps from Node Beta; making kits takes a facility of 600,000 cr), its way to the site, the fuel for its bunker, our stock hauled in. Deploy nothing yet: the site is chosen after this turn's reports, and you get the credits when it stands. (4) Keep making: the 125 circuit boards and 275 steel plate of the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk (51 boards held), which your builder builds at Node Beta when they are complete (the boards of the Lockbox come first); steel by hand from stored iron. (5) Keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral (the tool stock with view=reserve). A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Your haulers join the faction first (P5): the Lockbox and every outpost are theirs to fill.
Measure: the Lockbox at The Experiment built; the first outpost's build plan in your guidebook, with its routes written; circuit boards held against 125; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40; later: bases built, and bases supplied without a gap.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: your drones fight, hunt and guard (task 1). Hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16): since 2026-11-17 up to 45 ships of all divisions gather at Schedar Belt and up to 45 at Ironhollow's two belts: size the guard there, and say what a guard prevents and what it costs. What a kill yields and the reserve takes goes into storage where no bid stands for it. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates are yours (rule F27): find them and write each reading down (observe.py add pirate <system id> ...). The one-drone test is flown: no kill, and the drone left when two more pirates came. Next (Suggi, 2026-11-17): the test is a strike group of ten drones, not three. The ten are fitted as the test showed and form up in our own space first: a game fleet holds three, so how ten enter one battle on one side is yours to read and to prove there. Then they go where your record shows pirates alone or in small groups, never into a stronghold's system. They fight where three pirates or fewer stand; where more stand, or a fourth joins, the ten leave together. A drone leaves under 60 percent hull; the group breaks off when three of the ten have left or one is lost. If the pirates are too strong for ten, every ship of ours avoids pirates until we are stronger, and you say what stronger takes (hull, weapon, numbers). All by scripts (rule F29), the fits inside rule F12; no group of three is flown. Your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our outposts and stations; plan the guard of the first outpost before it is founded (task 3). Find why three of the first four grazer hunts did not reach their herds.
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour (surplus.py reserve names them; ore the fleet holds in plenty does not count); drones at work, 50 of 50, and of them on hunts, guards and tests; ships lost: 0 outside the strike test, and in it each loss with its cause; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand and what they are; the strike group of ten: the place, the pirates met, ticks, damage dealt and taken, kills, what a kill paid, what the test cost, and the verdict: fight or avoid.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time, on templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to every hour, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. For your next two turns (Suggi, 2026-11-17): ten scouts explore as far out from our safe space as they can reach, in every direction, and read every system on the way and at the far end, each read filed the same turn; the other twenty do the normal work. After those two turns about ten explore again, the three regions of D4 and the roads to them first (rule F26). Your scouts hunt as well (D8): where a herd lies on a scout's way it hunts, inside rule F9. For tasks 2 and 3, read the systems 3 or 4 jumps beyond our border that P4 (a) names, Felis and Intercrus first: the places without a station, the fields, the herds, who is there, where pirates stand, and the road to each; then say which serves best. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: a field of uranium ore first (every station bids 600 cr for it; requests ENT-194), then caustic sac and silicon ore. Write down where pirates stand, for Military (observe.py add pirate ...): your scouts look and leave (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn; the stance of rule F2 holds for them.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, 10 of 30, and for the two turns the farthest system read in each direction and the systems read for the first time; systems read in the three regions and on the roads to them.

## [ENT-034] | 2026-11-17 08:43 UTC | Unity | order | ref: governance/rulebook.md | see: ENT-033
