Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; faction members of 50 and the treasury's days of rent; the builder readied and the Lockbox at The Experiment begun; the site of the first outpost chosen and its build given to Logistics; Class Cards in five guidebooks; the orders no longer than the entry before, or the reason in its `Changed:` line.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore where a field has them, then iron. Weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. Fields beyond our space are yours to test and to widen (task 4 a). The stopgap of 2026-11-17 05:52 UTC is yours to reset: three lifts beyond the border (Schedar Belt to 45 ships, Ironhollow's two belts to 25 and 20) and the floors of four home fields lowered; a floor is there so that a field grows back. The iron belts you proposed for an outpost lie two jumps out, too close by rule F33: say with Intel which system 3 or 4 jumps out holds the fields Mining needs most (task 3).
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective: units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (rule F31); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered, inside rule F12: carbon ore at about 4 cr as far as Logistics' boards use it; silicon ore only at a price you judge fair against a field still to be found. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. A station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit. Price gaps between two stations (arbitrage) are yours to work as the books allow (D8): read both ends within the hour, one ship and one unit first. The hauling of fuel and supplies for our bases is Logistics'; what a base must buy, you buy.
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: you are the fleet's quartermaster and, from this entry, its base builder (D8, rule F16): you build our outposts, stations and facilities, make what they take, haul their fuel and supplies and keep them running. The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates the credits and materials (rule F33); inside that the build is yours. (1) Research the builder's class (task 1): the official pages on stations, factions and base building, the help of spacemolt_facility, the tables `facilities` and `facility_materials`, the tool base_plan. Your guidebook says how an outpost is deployed and supplied, what a facility at a foreign station costs to keep, and what a station of our own draws every cycle. (2) Your first build is the Faction Lockbox at The Experiment Research Station (P5). Choose one hauler as the fleet's builder. This turn it carries 200 steel plate and 50 circuit boards from Node Beta into its own storage at The Experiment and stays docked there. At 09:30 UTC the Fleet Admiral takes the hauler that holds them there into the faction, gives it the rights to build and the fee, and starts the build with it (spacemolt_facility faction_build, facility_type faction_lockbox), so that it stands at your next turn; if the game gives a member no such right, her flagship builds from what your hauler hands over. Every later build is yours by script. (3) Plan the first outpost as routes (task 3): the kit (two on offer at 74,010 cr at a player faction's station in lhs_1140, six jumps from Node Beta; making kits takes a facility of 600,000 cr), its way to the site, the fuel for its bunker, our stock hauled in. Deploy nothing yet: the site is chosen after this turn's reports, and you get the credits when it stands. (4) Keep making: the 125 circuit boards and 275 steel plate of the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk (51 boards held), which your builder builds at Node Beta when they are complete (the boards of the Lockbox come first); steel by hand from stored iron. (5) Keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral (the tool stock with view=reserve). A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Your haulers join the faction first (P5): the Lockbox and every outpost are theirs to fill.
Measure: the Lockbox at The Experiment built; the first outpost's build plan in your guidebook, with its routes written; circuit boards held against 125; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40; later: bases built, and bases supplied without a gap.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: your drones fight, hunt and guard (task 1). Hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16): since 2026-11-17 up to 45 ships of all divisions gather at Schedar Belt and up to 45 at Ironhollow's two belts: size the guard there, and say what a guard prevents and what it costs. What a kill yields and the reserve takes goes into storage where no bid stands for it. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates: no test and no fight for now (D9). No drone seeks them, a hunt leaves a place where one stands, a guard leaves when one attacks, and no fit is bought for a pirate group (the fit route left your list First by my hand on 2026-11-17). What the census and the public record show of them stays on file (observe.py), so that our ships avoid them and we know them when we are stronger. Your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our outposts and stations; plan the guard of the first outpost before it is founded (task 3). Find why three of the first four grazer hunts did not reach their herds.
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour (surplus.py reserve names them; ore the fleet holds in plenty does not count); drones at work, 50 of 50, and of them on hunts, guards and tests; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand, from the readings alone.
