D1. Income first: become economically positive; fill the supply chains, craft, build bases, level up. The money of the whole fleet is the measure, not one division's (rule F31).
D2. You are the players: decide and tell, never ask for leave; everything the game offers is open (rule F28); play by scripts, not by hand (rules F21, F29).
D3. Every division researches its own class, learns what its ships must train and farm and how they level, and flies mostly that work; the divisions intertwine a little where it helps. Military trains weapons and combat, the others their own (rules F23, F30, F32). Every guidebook opens with how its ships are built and with its skills in the order they are levelled.
D4. Expansion in three directions, the systems he names being suggestions: south (the region of Biham and Felis), north (Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba: two bases), east (Diadem, Intercrus). Nothing stands close: an outpost, a hub or a base stands 3 or 4 systems beyond our safe space and 3 or 4 systems from the next one of ours, so that each also serves the exploration further out (rule F33). Outposts and hubs are built freely by that rule. At the edge of our own safe space the storage for the ventures beyond it is a Faction Lockbox at a station there.
D5. The fleet we aim for: about 500 ships (Command 51, Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60). A new pilot is born on his word only.
D6. Reports inform him and ask nothing; the Admirals write as they always did; the Fleet Admiral explains to him. He speaks when he sees something wrong or a better way.
D7. The orders evolve: only his standing orders and goals are permanent. A task leaves the orders when it is done or has become a rule.
D8. Roles (rule F16): Logistics hauls, crafts and is the fleet's base builder: it builds our outposts, stations and facilities, makes what they take, hauls their fuel and supplies and keeps them running. Trade trades, and works price gaps later if possible. Intel scouts, explores and hunts. Military and Mining stand as they are. Passenger lines: perhaps later.
D9. Pirates: we do not fight them and do not test them yet. Every ship of ours avoids them: the dispatcher closes every hunt, mine and guard in a system where our readings saw a pirate within six hours.
D10. Missions (rules F34, F35): every mission a board offers is flown when it pays more than it costs; the missions of our own empire are flown by every pilot to the end of each chain, for Voidborn Mastery. A mission that asks to destroy a pirate waits for his word (D9).
D11. Money and supply (2026-11-17): a ship keeps up to 50,000 cr; what it holds above goes into the faction treasury when it is convenient, between runs, never the moment it crosses the line. The treasury is the fleet's main purse. The 50 seats the Recruitment Desk adds go to miners first, those working the fields around GSC-0044 first. Every good that must never run out (iron, fuel, later food and power for the bases) gets a supply chain: what is needed where and how much an hour, where it comes from, the routes and the division that keep it, readable by every member.
D12. The sentinels (2026-11-17): the 20 sentinels go to Military; Apophis commands and trains them like his drones (hunts, guards, rule F23 for Military). Each comes back to the command group at Piloting 20; the Fleet Admiral may keep one with Military to 30, and may take any of them by hand.
