## [ENT-039] | 2026-11-17 18:20 UTC | Unity | order | ref: game/fleet/division.py | see: ENT-038
Changed: the same turn of 17:30 UTC, a quarter of an hour after ENT-038. The sentinels stand in Military's plan since 18:20 UTC (D12; commits c531471 and e0b7403): built in this turn after all. The treasury holds the Desk's fee (365,079 cr at 18:20 UTC, requests ENT-242). Two words of Suggi's from my chat of about 18:00 and 18:10 UTC join as D13 and D14 (word for word in DECISIONS D227 and D228; queue ENT-175 asked Military for the group test). Longer than ENT-038 by those two lines.
In force from this entry until a newer one.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
STANDING GOALS (Suggi, 2026-11-16, about 22:05 UTC; his words with the spelling set right, as he asked on 2026-11-17; set and changed by him alone)
G1. The empire: "Your goal as Unity is to build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy, The Collective, and to be the most powerful presence in the game as The Collective. Standing goal."
G2. The grand fleet: "I want you to have the overall goal to get to the faction limit of 1,000, so we can build the grand fleet. This will take time: you have to do the levels 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and so on."
G3. The stations: "Explore the territory between the Frontier empire and our own. We build one or two stations in the area in between: maybe Yildun, and Aldebaran can be one too. That way we have two station locations. Also Hawthorne and Alchiba: that region can be another station. So in the medium to long term we expand to that region and keep the systems from our starting place to there all running with profitable routes and things to do."
G4. Mastery: each member "has an overarching goal to improve, learn and master their class": Military all things military, Trade trading and all its aspects, Intel scouting, Mining and Logistics theirs, the Fleet Admiral the fleet as a whole.
DIRECTION (Suggi's words since, in short; set and changed by him alone)
D1. Income first: become economically positive; fill the supply chains, craft, build bases, level up. The money of the whole fleet is the measure, not one division's (rule F31).
D2. You are the players: decide and tell, never ask for leave; everything the game offers is open (rule F28); play by scripts, not by hand (rules F21, F29).
D3. Every division researches its own class, learns what its ships must train and farm and how they level, and flies mostly that work; the divisions intertwine a little where it helps. Military trains weapons and combat, the others their own (rules F23, F30, F32). Every guidebook opens with how its ships are built and with its skills in the order they are levelled.
D4. Expansion in three directions, the systems he names being suggestions: south (the region of Biham and Felis), north (Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba: two bases), east (Diadem, Intercrus). Nothing stands close: an outpost, a hub or a base stands 3 or 4 systems beyond our safe space and 3 or 4 systems from the next one of ours, so that each also serves the exploration further out (rule F33). Outposts and hubs are built freely by that rule. At the edge of our own safe space the storage for the ventures beyond it is a Faction Lockbox at a station there.
D5. The fleet we aim for: about 500 ships (Command 51, Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60). A new pilot is born on his word only.
D6. Reports inform him and ask nothing; the Admirals write as they always did; the Fleet Admiral explains to him. He speaks when he sees something wrong or a better way.
D7. The orders evolve: only his standing orders and goals are permanent. A task leaves the orders when it is done or has become a rule.
D8. Roles (rule F16): Logistics hauls, crafts and is the fleet's base builder: it builds our outposts, stations and facilities, makes what they take, hauls their fuel and supplies and keeps them running. Trade trades, and works price gaps later if possible. Intel scouts, explores and hunts. Military and Mining stand as they are. Passenger lines: perhaps later.
D9. Pirates: we do not fight them and do not test them yet. Every ship of ours avoids them: the dispatcher closes every hunt, mine and guard in a system where our readings saw a pirate within six hours.
D10. Missions (rules F34, F35): every mission a board offers is flown when it pays more than it costs; the missions of our own empire are flown by every pilot to the end of each chain, for Voidborn Mastery. A mission that asks to destroy a pirate waits for his word (D9).
D11. Money and supply (2026-11-17): a ship keeps up to 50,000 cr; what it holds above goes into the faction treasury when it is convenient, between runs, never the moment it crosses the line. The treasury is the fleet's main purse. The 50 seats the Recruitment Desk adds go to miners first, those working the fields around GSC-0044 first. Every good that must never run out (iron, fuel, later food and power for the bases) gets a supply chain: what is needed where and how much an hour, where it comes from, the routes and the division that keep it, readable by every member.
D12. The sentinels (2026-11-17): the 20 sentinels go to Military; Apophis commands and trains them like his drones (hunts, guards, rule F23 for Military). Each comes back to the command group at Piloting 20; the Fleet Admiral may keep one with Military to 30, and may take any of them by hand.
D13. A route that does not pay rests, never for good (2026-11-17): 6 hours, then 24 when it loses again on its next opening, then 72 at most, by its record; it wakes early when what it rests on comes back (its bid, its field, its herd). A hand sets `off` only for danger or a route replaced, with the reason. Work that brings goods the reserve or a build needs, or the skills of the role (rule F23), is standing work and never rests for its wallet.
D14. Groups (2026-11-17): Military, and Intel's scouts when it serves, may hunt in groups of 3, 5, 10, 20 and more (a game fleet: the leader flies and fights for all), to take prey one ship cannot, and later pirates. Rule F9 (prey of 100 hull or less) holds for a ship alone; a group hunts up to what its measured runs show it kills. Pirates stay closed (D9) until Suggi opens them.
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (rules F8, F23, F30).
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work, most of each division on work of its own class.
P2. An economy that earns (rules F22, F25, F31, F34). A route gets no more ships than its bid, its field or its herd carries. The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space, what we craft, the surplus sold, and every mission that pays. The money above 50,000 cr a ship gathers in the treasury (D11): this turn by my hand, from my next turn by the five-minute pass.
  Goal: the money of the whole fleet rising hour by hour; the fleet's steady income per hour above zero.
P3. Level and upgrade (rule F17): Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open the hull tiers 2 to 5; a tier-1 hull asks no Piloting (the table `hulls`).
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its class gained and its next hull step.
P4. Outposts, hubs and bases (G3, D4, rule F33). The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates what it takes; Logistics builds, supplies and keeps it; Military guards it; Intel reads it first. (a) CLTV Outpost GSC-0044 stands: 3 systems beyond our border, 6 jumps from home; a Faction Lockbox and a Faction Fuel Bunker inside, no rent; members only. Now it must work: a hauler unloads into its storage and refuels at its bunker (one ship, one hold first), Mining gathers the fields one jump around it, Military guards it and the roads, Intel reads it and its six neighbours every hour. (b) The next sites: north (two bases, Aldebaran to Alchiba) and south (Biham, Felis), each 3 or 4 systems out (rule F33); Intel names the candidates with their fields, lanes and pirate record, I choose. (c) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr; the reserve gathers toward it and is never sold (rule F24).
  Goal: the outpost's storage and bunker used by our runs; candidates for the next two sites read.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own. Three offices stand at stations of others: Faction Lockboxes at Node Beta Industrial Station and The Experiment Research Station, and the Hiring Board at Node Beta (cap 50, full). Their rent, about 14,100 cr a day, comes from the treasury, which also pays every faction fee it holds whole. Next: the Recruitment Desk at Node Beta (cap 100): 300,000 cr from the treasury (my word is given), 200 plate and 100 boards, which Logistics holds there. The whole ladder: governance/empire.md.
  Goal: the Desk built and 100 members, miners first (D11); the treasury's days of rent after the fee.
TURN (the tasks of now; each leaves the orders when it is done)
None beyond the blocks below.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by. You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play). Plan your division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division"; sell what your ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes; build what you lack (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); answer the requests addressed to you; claim what you fill (admirals.log, rule F25: the filler now leaves another division's claimed routes alone). Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, judge your own routes, levels and results before you report them, and name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better and one you do not master yet.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F35); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13), the reserve (F24) and where we build (F33). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the empire with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through. Every turn: read the Admirals' reports and Feedback and fix what they name or open the door; answer every request before their next turn; keep the database, the catalog, the rules digest, the money figures, the tools and the station right; write the fleet report and lean orders (D7). Keep the treasury filled and the money above 50,000 cr a ship flowing into it (D11); pay the faction's steps (P5); choose where each outpost and base stands and allocate what it takes (P4, rules F31 and F33); check every guidebook's Class Card; read how many ships hold each level of Voidborn Mastery (rule F35); decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date. At my next turn: the five-minute sweep into the treasury (D11), a losing route's rest by its record (D13), the dispatcher's half of a group hunt (D14), the sentinels' money under Military on the station, then the supply chains (D11). Bring to him only what is his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list.
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; the Desk and faction members against its cap of 100; the treasury's days of rent; the orders no longer than the entry before, or the reason in its `Changed:` line.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore where a field has them, then iron. Weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. Fields beyond our space are yours to test and to widen through `lift_to`. (1) The outpost at GSC-0044 stands (P4 a): plan the gather fields one jump around it (Botein, Acrux, Atlas, Intercrus and its own two belts); when the Desk stands, the miners on those fields take the new faction seats first (D11), so that they may dock, store and refuel there. (2) The Liminal ordered at Node Beta: switch its pilot into it and read its gather runs against the Threshold's; a second hull only on that figure. (3) Deep Core Prospecting: read its RESULT line and give it to as many miners as Markab's platinum carries.
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective: units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (rule F31); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where. Where the bids are thin, keep the standing sell order you test with one ship and one good. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered, inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open: at 18:00 UTC 12 traders waited on full gather fields (I lifted gather-okab-belt to 42; yours to keep or undo). Price gaps between two stations are yours to work as the books allow (D8): read both ends within the hour, one ship and one unit first. Missions (D10, rule F34): you keep the missions that pay once for the fleet: for each, its route or what it lacks. What a base must buy, you buy; its hauling is Logistics'. Your reservecheck rule now stands in the filler (requests ENT-240).
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; once-missions flown and credits collected; traders at work, 40 of 40; crafting XP of the traders.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: you are the fleet's quartermaster and its base builder (D8, rule F16): you build our outposts, stations and facilities, make what they take, haul their fuel and supplies and keep them running. The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates the credits and materials (rule F33); inside that the build is yours, by routes and scripts. (1) The Recruitment Desk: my word is given, the treasury pays its 300,000 cr (build.py --treasury-ok; requests ENT-234): read the treasury in `info` before the call, build it at your turn, and your report names what it cost and the cap after. (2) The outpost at GSC-0044 (P4 a): test with one hauler and one hold that a run unloads into its faction storage and refuels at its bunker, then keep its bunker filled; your report names its base id and what the bunker holds. (3) Keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral (the tool stock with view=reserve). A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open.
Measure: the Desk built, with its cost; the outpost's storage and bunker used by a run of ours; inputs of the reserve with a known source; haulers at work, 40 of 40; later: bases supplied without a gap.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: your drones fight, hunt and guard (rule F23). Hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16), and the outpost at GSC-0044 and its roads (P4 a). What a kill yields and the reserve takes goes into storage where no bid stands for it. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. The escort stands whole: a guard route whose `guards` names a circuit follows the pilot that flies it (requests ENT-237; not flown by the dispatcher yet): write one for Logistics' runs to the outpost and read its first RESULT line. The filler no longer puts work in front of a guard ship (ENT-241). The sentinels (D12): they stand in your plan file since 18:20 UTC, each on the hunts it had and @hunter; plan and train them like your drones until Piloting 20 (they hold 6 to 8). With two roles in your division, `fill.py add` and `drop` take `--role drone` or `--role sentinel`. Groups (D14): one group of three drones by hand on a pilot whale near home: does the leader count in the three, do the members join the hunt, the kill, the loot, the damage per ship; your report names it, and I build the dispatcher's half from it. Pirates: no test, no group and no fight for now (D9): a hunt leaves a place where a pirate stands, a guard leaves when one attacks, no fit is bought for a pirate group. What the census and the public record show of them stays on file (observe.py). Your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our outposts and stations.
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour; drones and sentinels at work, 70 of 70, and of them on hunts, guards and tests; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; the first escort's RESULT line; the group test's record; the sentinels' Piloting toward 20.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time, on templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to every hour, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. About ten scouts explore: the north (Aldebaran to Alchiba) and the south (Biham, Felis) first, for the next sites (P4 b): name in your report the candidates 3 or 4 systems out with their fields, lanes and pirate record. Your scouts hunt as well (D8): where a herd lies on a scout's way it hunts, inside rule F9. Keep GSC-0044 and its six neighbours read hourly. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: a field of uranium ore first, then caustic sac and silicon ore. Write down where pirates stand, for Military (observe.py add pirate ...): your scouts look and leave (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn; the stance of rule F2 holds for them.
